INT. GRANDMOTHER'S SEWING ROOM - AFTERNOON

A narrow room at the back of a Calabrian apartment, sun slanting through yellowed lace. Bolts of fabric lean in corners like tired dancers. A black Singer sits under a crocheted cloth, a pincushion shaped like a tomato beside it.

ROSALBA GRIECO, 80, tiny, black dress, hair in a white bun, kneels on a cushion pinning a hem. CARMELINA GRIECO, 50s, her daughter, stands on the fitting stool in a half-finished wedding dress, arms out like a scarecrow. ISOTTA, 22, the granddaughter, sits cross-legged on the bed, picking at the chenille.

ROSALBA
You've lost weight.

CARMELINA
I haven't.

ROSALBA
The hips are loose.

CARMELINA
It's the way you cut it.

ROSALBA
I cut it for the bride. Not for you. For Isotta.

ISOTTA
It's not my dress, Nonna.

ROSALBA
Of course it is. You're the one marrying.

ISOTTA
I'm not marrying anyone.

CARMELINA
Isotta.

ISOTTA
I told Lorenzo in March. I told you both in April. You pretended you'd misheard.

ROSALBA
(through pins)
I heard.

CARMELINA
Mamma, you did not.

ROSALBA
I heard. I said to myself: she'll change her mind by June.

ISOTTA
It's August.

ROSALBA
So I made the dress in August.

CARMELINA
You made it for me to wear!

ROSALBA
For you to stand in. So I could see how it hangs.

ISOTTA
That is the saddest thing I have ever heard.

CARMELINA
It's not sad, it's how she is. She sews against reality.

ROSALBA
Where do you want me to put the lace?

Carmelina steps off the stool. The skirt catches on a pin. A thin tearing sound.

Rosalba holds up the torn hem to the window light. She does not look at either of them.
